  "raw": " the first studio album to be recorded by a girl group, and the first to use female producers.\n\nAs a result, A Night at the Opera is frequently credited as one of the all-conquering, female-driven pop albums, in the same polka-bag-pleasantbut-conveyingly-not-serious vein as Nanci Griffith and the Decades. But listen closely to its 14 tracks and the notion of its big band , orchestral-style arrangements might be more debatable. Such rarities as \"Long Tall Sally\", spoof movies songs \"MacArthur Park\" and the Abbey Road-style chunky blues \"Carsoline\" were clearly written by a band of 14 guys, most of whom presumably aren't composers, although Greg Howe, the all-age bassist, added some singing fills.\n\nIndeed, aside from the requisite ballads for obvious reasons, A Night at the Opera came charged with and celebrated the spirit of the",
